WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
(upbeat music)

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.420
And now, here's something neat

3
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.660
that I wanted you all to see.

4
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.930
Take a look.

5
00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:08.763
Hi, Drew.

6
00:00:08.763 --> 00:00:10.490 line:15%
My name is Fernanda, and in celebration

7
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.480 line:15%
of Hispanic Heritage Month,

8
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.790 line:15%
I'm gonna talk to you about the history

9
00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:15.270 line:15%
behind the iconic Catrinas that are often associated

10
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.220
with the other Los Muertos in Mexico.

11
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.920
The first ever Catrina was actually

12
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.150
a political cartoon by José Guadalupe Posada,

13
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.920
and it was published in 1913,

14
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.800
during Mexican revolutionary times.

15
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.480
The original Catrina was drawn wearing a big hat,

16
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.650
heavy earrings and a gown that was fashionable

17
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.710
for women of the aristocracy of the time.

18
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.630
Since it was Mexican revolutionary times,

19
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.670
it was meant to be commentary on the hypocrisy of the rich,

20
00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.780
that no matter how big your hat is,

21
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:40.200
how fashionable your clothes are,

22
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.420
or how much money you have,

23
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.670
in the end, we are all just skeletons.

24
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.810
And this type of Catrina is still recreated

25
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.350
by artisans across Mexico,

26
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.090
and you can really buy these anywhere.

27
00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.860
Fast forward a couple decades,

28
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.740
the Catrina reemerged in pop culture

29
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.580
after legendary artist, Diego Rivera,

30
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.140
recreated it for one of his most iconic murals.

31
00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.260
Now this is mural Dream of a Sunday Afternoon

32
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.380
in Alameda Park, in the historic center of Mexico city.

33
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.230
He is painted as a child holding hands with a Catrina,

34
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.170
and behind him is painted his famous wife, Frida Kahlo.

35
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.220
And over time, these Catrinas began to be

36
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.700
combined with Dia de Los Muertos,

37
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.300
a day where we celebrate life, death, and life after death.

38
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.270
And I personally believe that these modern day recreations

39
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.490
of the Catrina still uphold the same message

40
00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.860
that José Guadalupe Posada was trying to tell.

41
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.330
It's a way of unifying humanity during

42
00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.430
Dia de Los Muertos, showing that in the end,

43
00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.390
we are all just skeletons.

44
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.590
I hope you enjoyed the little history lesson Drew,

45
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.110
and I hope to also see you rocking a Catrina

46
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.190
for Dia de Los Muertos this year.

47
00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.369
I hope that you enjoyed that as much as I did.

48
00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:41.952 line:15%
(upbeat music)

